%I A400112 #10 Sep 24 2026 11:08:31
%S A400112 1,0,4,20,266,6080,343354,42466268,11827271764,7034374012600
%N A400112 Number of self-avoiding lattice paths from (0,0) to (n,n) in an n X n square grid such that no two consecutive maximal straight segments have equal length.
%C A400112 This type of path relates to a pencil puzzle genre titled Unequal Length Maze, popularized by Erich Friedman in the early 2000s and appearing in Round 11 of the 2017 World Puzzle Championship.
%C A400112 a(0) = 1 counts the trivial path from (0,0) to itself.
%C A400112 For n > 0, a(n) is even: reflection along the line y=x pairs each solution beginning with a horizontal step with one beginning with a vertical step.
%H A400112 Erich Friedman, <a href="https://erich-friedman.github.io/puzzle/unequal/">Unequal Length Mazes</a>.
%e A400112 For n=5, one solution is:
%e A400112   *---*   *---*---*---E
%e A400112   |   |   |
%e A400112   *   *   *---*---*---*
%e A400112   |   |               |
%e A400112   *   *   *   *   *   *
%e A400112   |   |               |
%e A400112   *   *---*---*---*---*
%e A400112   |
%e A400112   *---*---*   *   *   *
%e A400112           |
%e A400112   S---*---*   *   *   *
%e A400112 Here S=(0,0) and E=(5,5). Its successive straight-segment lengths are 2,1,2,4,1,3,4,2,3,1,3 which include no equal lengths consecutively.
%Y A400112 Cf. A007764, A265132.
%K A400112 nonn,walk,hard,more,new
%O A400112 0,3
%A A400112 _Eric Fox_, Sep 18 2026
